Note = {11th International Conference on Intellectual Capital, Knowledge
   Management and Organisational Learning (ICICKM), Univ Sydney Business
   Sch, Sydney, AUSTRALIA, NOV 06-07, 2014},
Abstract = {Empirical evidence has shown that employees' engagement in innovation is
   crucial as an effective resource for SMEs to be more innovative.
   Therefore, it is important to understand what is needed to foster
   innovative employees within an organization. The present study examines
   the effects of organizational learning and work environment on the
   formation of employees innovative work behavior in SMEs in three
   selected cities of the East Coast Economic Region, Malaysia. A
   stratified sampling technique was used for the sample of 260 employees
   at micro and small-scale enterprises. The study involved three main
   constructs: organizational learning, work environment and innovative
   work behavior. Frequency, percentage, mean, t-test and multiple linear
   regressions were used in data analysis. Our analysis revealed innovative
   work behavior of SMEs at a moderate level. Moreover, the innovative work
   behavior at micro enterprises is significantly lower than small
   enterprises. The innovation outputs are made up of employees with high
   innovative work behavior. Overall, statistical evidence indicated that
   organizational learning and work environment have a significant
   influence on the formation of innovative work behavior. Thus, the SMEs
   should strive to raise their employees' innovative work behavior in
   order to improve the performance of organizational innovation and
   competitiveness.},
